A||||10||btoth||electrical anomaly??||||5||
Z||000000||btoth||07-22-2001||01:36 PM||||It seems pretty common for turn signals to move the volt meter back and forth a bit when they are on... but this last week, when I have my turn signals on, the oil guage ticks back and forth! At idle sometimes it moves it far enough to turn the oil light on (and this is with a new engine). This doesn't seem right.  Does anyone else's car do this?  Could it just be a simple short in the dash? Because this last week I've also been having a problem with my tach lights going out.<P>Thanks for any help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DecadenceR||07-22-2001||02:09 PM||||To me it sounds like a short. I'd try cleaning up all the contacts and all. Unless someone has better info.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||07-22-2001||02:16 PM||||Bad ground would be my guess, haven't noticed on the Fiero, but have on the old Cherokee.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-22-2001||02:59 PM||||In this order....<P>REad the tip I just posted about irratic performance and radio noise.<P>Check all the grounds. There are several on the battery and block. See if on signal does it more. check the wires for that side very carefully.||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||08-08-2001||11:31 AM||||Update:<P>I was outside the other day messing with the light bulbs in order to make sure that they all have a good contact with the circuit. And I taped the plastic circuit piece back up against the back of the gauge pod because the corners were starting to curl (again).  Anyway... all of my right-hand side indicator lights are working properly now.  But..... my tach light will not go on at all!!  And yesterday, every time I turned my lights on, my temp needle would shoot up to the right as far is it would go! How weird is that?<P>Edit:  Also, my oil guage stoped moving with my turn signal once I fixed the indicator lights.  And today my temp needle isn't doing anything funny.  I'm going to go hit my head against a wall for a while.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||08-08-2001||01:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>Update...yesterday, every time I turned my lights on, my temp needle would shoot up to the right as far is it would go! How weird is that?...I'm going to go hit my head against a wall for a while.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>My bet is on the flexcircuit contacting the wrong pins on the IP connectors again - as in, the panel lamps terminal touching the temperature gage contact?  I don't even know if those are adjacent contacts - just guessing.  BTW, don't jar the connectors in your head too much , either, or they won't work right, either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!   <BR>||||reg||3
Z||000006||btoth||08-08-2001||07:50 PM||||<B>Flexcicuit</B>?  Is that what it's really called? Is there any way to get a new one?  Or is there any known way to repair the connections?<P>BTW, does it just come right off if you remove all the wiring harnesses from the back of the guage cluster?<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||08-09-2001||08:05 AM||||*bump*||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||08-09-2001||10:18 AM||||Check the connectors on the back of the IC, sometimes the little contacts will peel off the mylar backing and get all out of whack. This happens if the plug has been removed and installed a few times.||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||08-09-2001||12:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>[b]Flexcicuit</B>?  Is that what it's really called? Is there any way to get a new one?...Or...any...way to repair the connections?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Sorry to get you all stirred up, but yes, we used to call those flexible PC board things "flexcircuits" when I worked for an electronics company years ago.  I don't know if new ones are available, but I doubt it.  Probably cheaper to get the whole dash from salvage, or repair by re-bonding the conductors so they stay put on the backing, etc.  I don't know any "secrets" for repairing flexcircuits - but you're no dummy  - you can figure something out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.     <P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000010||btoth||08-09-2001||04:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Sorry to get you all stirred up<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No I was serious.  It's always helpful to know what to properly call something.  'Till now I was refering to it as the "plastic circuit thingy"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> j/k<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>but you're no dummy  - you can figure something out   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.     <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm sure I will one of these days when it's not 96 degrees out or pouring rain.  Thanks for the info though.<P>||||reg||1
